Here is the MATLAB code to solve for "x":
syms a b c p q r x
eq1 = ap + bq + cr == 28;
eq2 = a + b + c == 10;
eq3 = x == a + 2*b - c/3;
sol = solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], [a, b, c]);
The "sol" variable will contain the solutions for "a", "b", and "c" that can be used to calculate the value of "x".

response:
To extract the values of "x" from the solution set, you can substitute the values of "a," "b," and "c" obtained from the sol command into the expression for "x." Here's how you can do it:

% Solving the system of equations using symbolic math toolbox
syms a b c p q r x
eq1 = ap + bq + cr == 28;
eq2 = a + b + c == 10;
eq3 = x == a + 2*b - c/3;
sol = solve([eq1, eq2, eq3], [a, b, c]);

% Extracting the values of 'x' using the subs function
x_values = subs(subs(subs(x, sym(sol.a), double(sol.a)), sym(sol.b), double(sol.b)), sym(sol.c), double(sol.c), 'direct');

% Displaying the values of 'x'
disp(x_values);

This code calculates the values of "a," "b," and "c" using the solve function and then uses the substitution (subs) function three times to replace the symbols with their corresponding numerical values. The resulting expressions are evaluated directly ('direct') using the double function. This step returns a vector containing all possible values of "x." Note that if there are multiple solutions, the subs function will return a cell array containing multiple vectors. In such cases, you may need to iterate over each solution vector to extract the individual values of "x."

