To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in WordPress when using Next.js, follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` middleware in your Next.js project by running the following command in your terminal:

   ```bash
   npm install cors
   ```

2. Import and use the `cors` middleware in your `server/middlewares.js` file:

   ```javascript
   import Cors from 'cors';

   const corsMiddleware = Cors({
     origin: process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS,
     optionsSuccessStatus: 200, // some legacy browsers (IE11, various SmartTVs) choke on 204
   });

   export default corsMiddleware;
   ```

3. In the `server/index.js` file, add the `corsMiddleware` to the list of middlewares that are executed before the Next.js app:

   ```javascript
   const middleware = [myMiddleware1, myMiddleware2, corsMiddleware];

   module.exports = {
     // ...
     serverRuntimeConfig: {
       // ...
     },
     middleware,
   };
   ```

4. Define the `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` environment variable with the URLs of the websites allowed to access your API:

   ```bash
   echo ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://example.com https://anotherexample.com >> .env.local
   ```

5. Restart your server or run `next start`.

Now, any requests made to your Next.js application from the allowed origins will be allowed by the CORS middleware, preventing the CORS error in WordPress.

Note: The `cors` middleware is used for development purposes only. In production, you can deploy your Next.js application to a server that supports CORS out of the box or use a CDN like Cloudflare that provides CORS support. This will help reduce the overhead of running an additional middleware in production.

