In a logical syllogism, there are two premises and one conclusion. The major premise makes a general statement about a group or class (in this case, all men), while the minor premise provides specific information about a particular individual or instance (in this case, Socrates). The conclusion follows logically from the combination of these two premises. In this example, the conclusion states that since Socrates is a man (minor premise) and all men are mortal (major premise), then Socrates must also be mortal (conclusion). This type of argument is called a syllogism because it has three parts or "members" (the two premises and the conclusion). Syllogisms can help us draw valid conclusions based on our knowledge and beliefs about the world around us.

